WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:09.024
This is Chapter Six, Episode Seven: the Realm Community, and Greenland.

3
00:00:09.574 --> 00:00:15.749
Denmark is in a realm community, rigsfællesskab, with Greenland and the Faroe Islands.

4
00:00:15.699 --> 00:00:21.574
The official name for this realm community is "Kongeriget Danmark", the Kingdom of Denmark.

5
00:00:21.574 --> 00:00:27.561
The constitution applies to all three parts of Denmark's realm, which has the king as head of state.

6
00:00:27.561 --> 00:00:32.154
The Faroe Islands and Greenland each elect two members to the Folketing.

7
00:00:32.704 --> 00:00:39.691
Greenland and the Faroe Islands pass their own laws in a long list of areas and have their own administration.

8
00:00:39.642 --> 00:00:49.054
In the other areas, the Folketing passes the laws also for Greenland and the Faroe Islands, and the administration lies with the Danish authorities.

9
00:00:49.054 --> 00:00:56.341
This applies, among other things, to foreign and security policy and to currency and monetary policy.

10
00:00:56.341 --> 00:01:08.820
In 2005, Greenland and the Faroe Islands got the possibility, each separately or together, to negotiate and enter into certain international agreements on behalf of the realm.

11
00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:10.945
Now, Greenland.

12
00:01:10.895 --> 00:01:12.645
Greenland's geography.

13
00:01:12.645 --> 00:01:18.570
Greenland, with 2.1 million square kilometres, is the world's largest island.

14
00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:25.230
It is about as big as Germany, France, the United Kingdom, Spain, Italy and Greece together.

15
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:33.042
Greenland is geographically part of the North American continent and lies far to the north, northeast of Canada.

16
00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:41.542
Greenland's area makes up 98 percent of the area of the entire realm community, Denmark, Greenland and the Faroe Islands.

17
00:01:41.542 --> 00:01:45.792
About 83 percent of Greenland is covered by ice all year.

18
00:01:45.792 --> 00:01:50.468
The biggest continuous area is called the Inland Ice, Indlandsisen.

19
00:01:50.468 --> 00:02:00.576
It covers 1.7 million square kilometres, or about 80 percent of Greenland's total area, and is up to 3,400 metres thick.

20
00:02:01.126 --> 00:02:10.226
Greenland's highest point is Gunnbjørn Fjeld, which lies in East Greenland, 3,694 metres above sea level.

21
00:02:10.176 --> 00:02:15.330
Gunnbjørn Fjeld is by the way also the world's highest point north of the Arctic Circle.

22
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:20.067
The roughly 57,000 inhabitants live along the coast.

23
00:02:20.017 --> 00:02:26.580
About 20,000 live in the capital Nuuk, also called Godthåb, which is Greenland's largest town.

24
00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:28.805
Greenland's history.

25
00:02:28.755 --> 00:02:31.392
Greenland has been inhabited for millennia.

26
00:02:31.392 --> 00:02:40.055
From about the year 2500 before Christ, smaller groups of Arctic hunter peoples immigrated in several rounds from Canada.

27
00:02:40.055 --> 00:02:46.667
Greenland's north, west and east coasts were all populated, and different hunter cultures arose.

28
00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:57.822
Finds of, among other things, animal bones show that the northern cultures were dependent on musk ox hunting, while the southern cultures lived on seal hunting and reindeer hunting.

29
00:02:58.372 --> 00:03:10.368
Today's Greenlandic people, the Inuit, immigrated from Alaska and Canada around the year 1200 after Christ to north-western Greenland and later spread to South Greenland.

30
00:03:10.918 --> 00:03:18.368
Archaeological finds show that Icelanders and Norwegians already in the 900s after Christ settled in Greenland.

31
00:03:18.318 --> 00:03:27.055
These so-called Norse people joined in the 1200s the Norwegian king and thereby followed into the Danish-Norwegian union from 1380.

32
00:03:27.055 --> 00:03:31.030
After that they disappeared from Greenland during the 1400s.

33
00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:37.668
Possibly also because climate changes with a colder climate as a result made it difficult to farm in Greenland.

34
00:03:37.668 --> 00:03:49.098
In the 1500s and 1600s, the Danish king sent a series of expeditions, but a permanent Danish presence in Greenland only took root in the 1700s.

35
00:03:49.648 --> 00:03:56.098
In 1721, the priest Hans Egede travelled to the island to find the Norse people again.

36
00:03:56.048 --> 00:04:09.785
When he did not find the Norse people, he settled to trade and make the Inuit Christian, and in 1728 he founded the colony Godthåb, which today is the capital of Greenland, in Greenlandic Nuuk.

37
00:04:09.785 --> 00:04:22.010
To promote the spread of Christianity in Greenland, Hans Egede also founded the Greenlandic written language, so that he could write down and publish texts from the Bible in the Greenlanders' own language.

38
00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:32.748
This early spread of the Inuit language in writing has had great importance for the language today being better preserved in Greenland than among the Inuit populations in Canada and Alaska.

39
00:04:33.298 --> 00:04:43.673
During the 1700s and 1800s, Denmark set up several colonies in Greenland, from which Greenlandic products were traded that were sold in Europe.

40
00:04:43.623 --> 00:04:53.223
This applied especially to blubber, fat, from seals and whales, which could be boiled into train oil, tran, an oil that was used for lighting.

41
00:04:53.223 --> 00:05:01.302
With the spread of electric lamps at the beginning of the 1900s, fishing became instead Greenland's most important industry.

42
00:05:01.852 --> 00:05:18.989
From 1776 to 1950, a state company, the Royal Greenland Trading Department, Den Kongelige Grønlandske Handel, had a monopoly, exclusive right, on all trade in Greenland and also administered the Greenlandic colonies.

43
00:05:18.989 --> 00:05:27.227
That meant the Greenlanders were only allowed to sell their goods to the Danish colony managers, who thereby secured control of the trade.

44
00:05:27.227 --> 00:05:42.127
Conversely, the Royal Danish Greenland Trade also had a duty to supply the small Greenlandic settlements with goods from outside, for example flour, groats, salt, sugar, knives, hunting weapons and cotton clothes.

45
00:05:42.127 --> 00:05:46.239
Greenland was in this period also a closed country for foreigners.

46
00:05:46.239 --> 00:05:55.992
Only persons with a special permit and task from the Danish state, for example as civil servants, craftsmen, doctors or priests, had access.

47
00:05:56.542 --> 00:06:02.779
During the Second World War, Greenland, unlike Denmark, was not occupied by the Germans.

48
00:06:02.729 --> 00:06:22.429
In violation of the German occupying power's, and thereby also the Danish government's, wishes, Denmark's ambassador in Washington DC, Henrik Kauffmann, in 1941 entered into an agreement with the USA which gave the Americans permission to set up military bases in Greenland.

49
00:06:22.429 --> 00:06:34.654
That was necessary, among other things, to protect and supply the inhabitants of Greenland with foodstuffs, building materials, medicine and fuel, which they had until then got from Denmark.

50
00:06:34.654 --> 00:06:46.866
The Americans wanted conversely to prevent other countries, especially Germany, occupying Greenland, and in addition the USA was to use minerals from Greenland's subsoil for its war production.

51
00:06:47.416 --> 00:06:55.141
After the Second World War, the USA continued to have military bases in Greenland by agreement with Denmark.

52
00:06:55.091 --> 00:07:07.491
At the beginning of the 1950s, the Americans built a base in Qaanaaq, Thule, in North Greenland, whereby the settlement of several Greenlandic families was moved to make room for the base.

53
00:07:07.491 --> 00:07:17.928
The base is the USA's northernmost military base, and there was only a short distance to the Soviet Union, and therefore it was central to the air defence of the USA and NATO.

54
00:07:17.928 --> 00:07:21.928
Earlier the base has been known as Thule Air Base or the Thule Base.

55
00:07:21.928 --> 00:07:27.852
In 2023, it was renamed Pituffik Space Base, and it is still used by the USA.

56
00:07:28.402 --> 00:07:31.002
Now it is time for your exam practice.

57
00:07:30.952 --> 00:07:35.864
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

58
00:07:35.864 --> 00:07:43.350
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

59
00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:45.150
Question 1.

60
00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:48.400
Which island group is part of the Danish Realm Community?

61
00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.087
Option A: The Shetland Islands.

62
00:07:51.087 --> 00:07:53.537
Option B: The Orkney Islands.

63
00:07:53.537 --> 00:07:56.184
Option C: The Faroe Islands.
[DA] Hvilken af følgende øgrupper er en del af det danske rigsfællesskab?  A: Shetlandsøerne  |  B: Orkneyøerne  |  C: Færøerne

64
00:08:02.734 --> 00:08:05.421
The answer is C: The Faroe Islands.

65
00:08:05.371 --> 00:08:09.498
It appeared in the June 2020 and June 2021 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Færøerne

66
00:08:10.048 --> 00:08:11.298
Question 2.

67
00:08:11.248 --> 00:08:13.785
Where does the USA have the Thule air base?

68
00:08:13.785 --> 00:08:15.873
Option A: On Bornholm.

69
00:08:15.873 --> 00:08:18.485
Option B: In the Faroe Islands.

70
00:08:18.485 --> 00:08:20.508
Option C: In Greenland.
[DA] Hvor har USA luftbasen Thulebasen?  A: På Bornholm  |  B: På Færøerne  |  C: I Grønland

71
00:08:27.058 --> 00:08:29.308
The answer is C: In Greenland.

72
00:08:29.258 --> 00:08:33.726
It appeared in the November 2021 and November 2024 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: I Grønland

73
00:08:34.276 --> 00:08:35.613
Question 3.

74
00:08:35.563 --> 00:08:37.838
What is Greenland's largest town called?

75
00:08:37.838 --> 00:08:39.463
Option A: Nuuk.

76
00:08:39.463 --> 00:08:41.463
Option B: Tórshavn.

77
00:08:41.463 --> 00:08:43.440
Option C: Reykjavik.
[DA] Hvad hedder Grønlands største by?  A: Nuuk  |  B: Tórshavn  |  C: Reykjavik

78
00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:51.740
The answer is A: Nuuk.

79
00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:55.866
It appeared in the May 2023 and May 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Nuuk

80
00:08:56.416 --> 00:08:57.716
Question 4.

81
00:08:57.666 --> 00:09:01.278
How large a share of Greenland is covered by ice all year?

82
00:09:01.278 --> 00:09:04.028
Option A: About 40 percent.

83
00:09:04.028 --> 00:09:06.641
Option B: About 60 percent.

84
00:09:06.641 --> 00:09:09.444
Option C: About 80 percent.
[DA] Hvor stor en andel af Grønland er dækket af is hele året?  A: Cirka 40 procent  |  B: Cirka 60 procent  |  C: Cirka 80 procent

85
00:09:15.994 --> 00:09:18.907
The answer is C: About 80 percent.

86
00:09:18.856 --> 00:09:23.430
It appeared in the November 2023 and June 2026 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Cirka 80 procent

87
00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:25.442
Question 5.

88
00:09:25.393 --> 00:09:28.668
Does the constitution apply in the Faroe Islands and Greenland?

89
00:09:28.668 --> 00:09:30.293
Option A: Yes.

90
00:09:30.293 --> 00:09:31.944
Option B: No.
[DA] Gælder grundloven på Færøerne og i Grønland?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

91
00:09:38.494 --> 00:09:40.282
The answer is A: Yes.

92
00:09:40.231 --> 00:09:44.538
It appeared in the May 2024 and November 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Ja

93
00:09:45.088 --> 00:09:47.651
That's the end of Part One of Episode 7.

94
00:09:47.601 --> 00:09:53.301
In Part Two, we continue with Greenland's self-government, economy and culture.

95
00:09:53.301 --> 00:09:54.348
See you there.

